WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:03.830
- [Interviewer] All right, Shonda.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:04.964
- [Shonda] Yes.

00:00:04.964 --> 00:00:05.797
- [Interviewer] Where'd you grow up?

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:06.630
Where are you from originally?

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.000
- I'm originally from Cincinnati Ohio.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
I grew up on weekends down
here in Leslie County.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.640
I moved back down here in '98.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.473
- [Interviewer] Tell me
about your childhood,

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:21.970
you had both your parents growing up?

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.120
- Yeah, I had both my parents growing up.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.883
As I was growing up it was pretty bad.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.673
Not really much bad.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.720
We lived downtown
Cincinnati on Elder street.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.370
My dad took me to school and
picked me up from school.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.372
Me and my brother was about
the only white children

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:43.930
in the classroom.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:44.913
- [Interviewer] Is that right?

00:00:45.924 --> 00:00:47.490
- I don't know if I can say that or not?

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:48.600
Sorry.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.130
- [Interviewer] The truth is the truth.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.585
- It was really not much of a childhood.

00:00:54.585 --> 00:00:56.993
I didn't have friends
of nothing to play with.

00:00:58.268 --> 00:01:00.400
- [Interviewer] Because you
were white were you excluded?

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.110
- Well, yeah that and
I started busing tables

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.180
at Ghost Star Chili in
Cincinnati with my mom.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.140
- [Interviewer] How far
did you go in school?

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.870
- I went to the 10th grade.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:12.840
- [Interviewer] 10th grade.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.523
Then you started working in a restaurant?

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.340
- I was working at age 12 with my mother

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.340
and then at 14 when I was
allowed to get the permit

00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:35.080
and then at 16 I worked at a
lot of restaurants up there

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.013
in Clifton and I worked at
White Castle in Covington.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.150
I worked at a strip club in Covington

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.633
and in Newport Kentucky.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.960
- [Interviewer] How was working
at a strip club for you?

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.810
- It's enjoyable.

00:01:56.435 --> 00:01:59.367
It was different than a restaurant.

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.127
- [Interviewer] I'm sure it was.

00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:02.851
Was the money good for you?

00:02:02.851 --> 00:02:05.140
- Yes, the money was good.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.203
I moved back down here in '98

00:02:07.203 --> 00:02:10.710
with my mom and my kids and stuff.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:15.710
She had two aneurysms ruptured
and she wasn't fully equipped

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.983
to take care of herself.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:22.110
She died of lung cancer in 2002.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.323
- [Interviewer] I'm sorry.

00:02:24.412 --> 00:02:25.762
- My sister was just eight.

00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:30.103
My oldest was nine and
my youngest was seven.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.553
- [Interviewer] Did
you raise your younger?

00:02:32.553 --> 00:02:35.693
- I was wild when I moved back down here.

00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:39.080
When my mom passed away I had to wake up.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.540
I was in and out of jail,
pretty much still am.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.373
I'm sorry.

00:02:46.741 --> 00:02:47.800
- [Interviewer] That's your past.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.800
- Down here, if you are
on medication, don't drive

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.580
because they will give
you a DUI no matter what.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.533
- [Interviewer] What kind of
medications have you been on?

00:02:58.533 --> 00:03:03.520
- I've been on pain medication
where I was taking seizures.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.093
I was on some Klonopin.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.970
Had to take my medication
every time I drove.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.000
It's like the state police of some county

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.413
would get me (laughs) every time.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.993
I just quit driving and start walking.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.600
- [Interviewer] You started
having children of your own

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.532
at what age?

00:03:22.532 --> 00:03:26.216
- I was 18 when I had my oldest
and 21 when I had my second.

00:03:26.216 --> 00:03:29.317
- [Interviewer] You have two kids?

00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:30.270
- Yes.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.750
- [Interviewer] Were you married?

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.050
- I've been married twice.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.460
My second husband, we're not divorced but

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.273
he's dying of cancer up north somewhere.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.193
I don't talk to him, we don't get along.

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:48.670
- [Interviewer] This is
one of the poorest parts

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:49.720
of the country.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.553
- Yes.

00:03:50.553 --> 00:03:52.030
- [Interviewer] Did you grow up very poor?

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:52.863
- Yeah.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.370
I didn't need much when I
was growing up or nothing

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.370
but as I got older and stuff like that,

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:05.786
this place right here, you
have to travel to work.

00:04:05.786 --> 00:04:10.170
In order to travel you got to
have money to have a vehicle

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.550
and money for gas and
stuff to be transportation.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.475
- [Interviewer] How do you
support yourself today?

00:04:15.475 --> 00:04:16.490
- I draw SSI.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:17.323
- [Interviewer] You do.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.156
How old are you?

00:04:18.156 --> 00:04:18.989
- Huh?

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:19.822
- [Interviewer] How old are you?

00:04:19.822 --> 00:04:20.739
- I'm 48.

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:22.322
- [Interviewer] 48.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.623
Looking back at your
life, do you have regrets?

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.010
- I regret it all.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.097
I regret coming back here
because I don't know.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.160
I've got an older brother
lives in Cincinnati

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.883
that I don't even get to
spend time with his children.

00:04:47.229 --> 00:04:51.163
I quit talking to him the day
we put my mom in the ground.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.510
I don't talk to him.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.733
- [Interviewer] Are drugs
a part of your life?

00:04:56.733 --> 00:04:59.340
- Drugs have been a part
of my life all my life.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.183
- [Interviewer] Ever
since the pain killers?

00:05:01.183 --> 00:05:02.016
- Yeah.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.943
I got in a bad relationship.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.570
I got scars and stuff on
my face where he hit me.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.930
My left ear is damaged where he kicked me,

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:18.930
I'm hard of hearing.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.723
Yes, drugs have been a lot in my life.

00:05:24.915 --> 00:05:27.515
So far right now it's just
been marijuana right now.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.170
- [Interviewer] Have you
traveled around the country?

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:31.003
- No.

00:05:31.003 --> 00:05:32.570
- [Interviewer] You've not
been outside of Cincinnati

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:33.590
and Kentucky?

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.105
- No.

00:05:35.105 --> 00:05:36.833
I would love to.

00:05:36.833 --> 00:05:38.420
- [Interviewer] Did you have
dreams when you were young

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.253
of a different life?

00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:41.916
- I had a lot of dreams but the main dream

00:05:41.916 --> 00:05:46.916
I wanted, I wanted to be able
to have a white picket fence

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.100
house and my children and my grand babies.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.320
I wanted a husband that
didn't cheat (laughs).

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.900
- [Interviewer] Do you
find it hard to survive?

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.750
Hard to make ends meet here?

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:09.946
- [Shonda] Yes.

00:06:09.946 --> 00:06:12.300
It's very hard.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.583
- [Interviewer] It's hard
for everyone it seems.

00:06:15.372 --> 00:06:17.480
- [Shonda] There's no
money flowing nowhere.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.870
You can't make nothing.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.620
- [Interviewer] How much do
you think you live on a year?

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:23.844
- Huh?

00:06:23.844 --> 00:06:24.963
- [Interviewer] How much
do you live on a year?

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.003
- [Shonda] Less than $10,000.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.290
- [Interviewer] You're
raising your kids too?

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.720
What was the best time in your life?

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.420
- [Shonda] What was the best time?

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:42.760
It's pretty weird.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.643
My mom was pregnant, she didn't know it,

00:06:44.643 --> 00:06:49.643
with my little sister and I
was pregnant at the same time.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.490
We worked at the same place, Kroger's,

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.013
in Hyde Park in Cincinnati.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.023
My mom was my best friend.

00:07:00.631 --> 00:07:03.923
She was my whole rock,
my whole everything.

00:07:05.829 --> 00:07:09.180
We went to tanning beds together
and we ate out together.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.590
When I stayed together
we had the girls with us,

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.940
all three of them, my
two and my little sister.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.293
It was always my mother.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:23.820
- [Interviewer] Are you religious?

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:24.653
- No.

00:07:24.653 --> 00:07:25.660
- [Interviewer] No.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:26.970
A lot of people out here are.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:27.803
- Yeah.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:33.284
- [Interviewer] Would you
describe yourself as happy?

00:07:33.284 --> 00:07:34.117
- Huh?

00:07:34.117 --> 00:07:35.583
- [Interviewer] Would you
describe yourself as happy?

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.920
- Yeah.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:39.753
I'm happy.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.920
My sister's getting really to have a baby

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.310
and she's due (indistinct)

00:07:50.819 --> 00:07:52.980
but I think she's gonna
wait until the day.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.670
It's already weighted eight pounds

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.023
so it's gonna come out a walking probably.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.233
That's her second baby.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.483
I'm happy my grandchildren are with me.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:06.340
I've got five.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.110
I've got three more that
live in Jackson county

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:10.760
with my daughter and her husband.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.523
I got to see them last
night, they're a handful.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:20.893
There's one that I don't get to see,

00:08:22.314 --> 00:08:24.860
that's not part of happiness.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.720
He's happy, that's all that matters to me.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.490
- [Interviewer] Why do you say?

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:34.490
- My youngest grandson, their
little brother, he's happy

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:37.993
so that's all that matters.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.060
- [Interviewer] What emotions
do you have to deal with most

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:42.893
in your life?

00:08:42.893 --> 00:08:44.740
Do you get depressed or anxious or angry?

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:46.943
- I get depressed a lot.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.127
Most of the time on
bills when (indistinct).

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.250
Not again.

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:02.163
It's not even been 31 days, but
some months it's pretty bad.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.080
- [Interviewer] What's been
the worst time of your life?

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:05.913
- Huh?

00:09:05.913 --> 00:09:08.010
- [Interviewer] What's been
the hardest time of your life?

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.823
The most difficult thing
you've gone through?

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:11.582
Losing your mom?

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.800
- I'm still struggling with it
because I talked to my father

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.563
a few hours before he passed away.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.257
I was supposed to see him the next morning

00:09:24.257 --> 00:09:28.283
and I want answers, I wanna know why.

00:09:30.765 --> 00:09:31.598
It's so quick.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:38.200
He's been sick a long time and
he just didn't tell nobody.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.700
He wanted to be the macho man
to take care of everything

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.702
and he did.

00:09:44.702 --> 00:09:46.990
He don't want no one to
touch his lawn mower,

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:48.313
he did it on his own.

00:09:51.237 --> 00:09:54.830
I still wonder why?

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:55.663
- [Interviewer] Why what?

00:09:55.663 --> 00:09:57.900
- Why did it take him so quick?

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:58.890
- [Interviewer] How old was
your father when he passed?

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.870
- He had cancer but he never
went to the cancer doctor.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.700
He never went and got treatment.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:09.700
He just let it live up on him
and I guess it took the toll.

00:10:13.956 --> 00:10:15.270
He had a bad heart.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.470
- [Interviewer] Some people
out here just don't believe

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:17.770
in going to doctors.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:18.922
- Huh?

00:10:18.922 --> 00:10:19.755
- [Interviewer] Some people
out here just don't believe

00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:20.830
in going to doctors?

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:21.663
- Yeah.

00:10:23.038 --> 00:10:28.038
I haven't been in over a year.

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:33.430
I've had a stroke and the
high blood pressure medicine

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:36.873
that the doctor give me
is what caused my stroke.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:39.843
The dog.

00:10:40.695 --> 00:10:43.373
That is what caused it.

00:10:43.373 --> 00:10:46.080
I've not been but I need to go back.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.643
My plans are to go back to the doctor.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:51.920
- [Interviewer] What's
the most important lesson

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.423
you've learned in your life?

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:00.420
- Don't take from others.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:07.043
Respect and family.

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:09.333
Family means a lot.

00:11:11.396 --> 00:11:13.330
- [Interviewer] What do you
see for the future of your kids

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:15.920
growing up here in the deep Appalachians?

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.220
- Right now I see nothing.

00:11:18.448 --> 00:11:22.950
If they don't get this
pandemic thing going

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.560
and getting the kids back in school

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:27.393
where they can learn.

00:11:27.393 --> 00:11:29.220
When they're at home they
think they can just play,

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.400
they're home.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.450
They think everything's
supposed to be like

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.800
when they're out of school.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:40.043
I have a time with them too
and try to get on their work.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.560
I don't see nothing.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.070
To me it's like a walking dead.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:51.413
That's how I feel.

00:11:51.413 --> 00:11:53.900
We're all just gonna be
like the walking dead.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.998
No sense in burying us
because we're gonna come back.

00:11:58.998 --> 00:12:00.840
I don't know.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.673
- [Interviewer] All right Shonda.

00:12:01.673 --> 00:12:03.590
Thank you so much for talking with me

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:07.020
and good luck with your
kids and everything else

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.460
going on in your life.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:09.310
- [Shonda] I thank you.

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:10.343
- [Interviewer] Thank you.

